d be to see the  molecular world of neuroscience which is  an  entire very interesting field and the  systems world of neuroscience where  people focus more on the circuits and  the electrical activities  to see them merge in part driven by  tools but in part driven by what  insights we can learn  from whole brain imaging  um one person had a question about  mapping which i think  what you're just talking about feeds  into that question really  well they wanted to know about mapping  in worms worm brain  human brain mouse brain and of course  some things are very well mapped out  right like the worm brain we know where  every neuron is going to go and the  basic shape it's going to  make but that doesn't  give you insight into how much things  can vary from worm to worm  at an individual level based upon  experience for example  so could you talk about what you think  the fundamental differences are between  mapping  big brains and the worm brain  sure so we picked the species we worked  on quite carefully  the worm has 302 neurons and the  connections are  are somewhat stereotype from worm to  worm we now know there's some  variability  but um but one big question is how  variable are they  and this is where the expansion method  becomes very useful is that so now  people would spend years to reconstruct  one worm or a couple worms  they would cut the worm into literally  10 000 sections  and image them with expensive electron  microscopes  but just this past summer we showed that  we could expand an entire worm  and image it using an inexpensive  ordinary microscope  so my hope is by the end of this year we  will show that we can color code  the cells of the worm expand it and make  maps on a worm to worm bases  now a zebrafish a larval zebrafish has  about a hundred thousand neurons  ballpark a thousand times more than the  worm  a mouse has about a thousand times more  than the larval zebrafish  and a human has ballpark about a  thousand times more than the mouse  so i hope we can do with small brains is  to map many of them  and we can ask questions like how does  the wiring differ from brain to brain  how do the cell types differ do those  differences  associate with differences in the  computations and behaviors  my hope is we can build theories of  brain function  we can then test these because we have  many collaborating  students who work between our group and  other groups at the mcgovern institute  and beyond  we can test those ideas through  perturbations of the mouse spring  the problem with the mouse brain of  course is it's too big to image the  whole thing right now  and even making a map of every uh  connection in the mouse brain people  speculate it could cost between 100  million and 1 billion  us dollars so you know we probably we  want to work a bit harder on the  technology and make it better before  spending that much money  on one mouse brain so in summary let's  make maps of many small brains  let's make maps of part of larger brains  like mice and humans  we can make theories of how neurons work  together using small brains but then  test them on the larger brains  and see what general principles of brain  function emerge  thank you another question that's come  in that i think  uh is such an important one and you  probably also have some experience with  your physics background where  experimentalists and theorists are  very distinct fields whereas in biology  the person's question is do you think  the next  big breakthrough will will come from  understanding new methods of acquiring  experimental data  or methods of analyzing the experimental  data that we're already able to acquire  yeah it's a good question we obviously  need both  and uh it's great that we have so many  fantastic experimental as well as  computational colleagues at  the mcgovern institute and and  throughout mit  um here's why i think we need both  if you don't see or control a biological  system with sufficient resolution  you might be missing out and right now  most of us in neuroscience think of the  brain cell  as a fundamental unit of computation but  there are others like mark harnett at  the mcgovern institute who work on how  parts of cells  compute how do we know for sure what  detail to ignore  and what detail is necessary so being  able to  image and control with enough resolution  is very  very important now on the other hand the  more resolution the more detail  the more things there are to analyze as  well  and so in the coming years our hope is i  think to make connections with machine  learning and ai  with different statistical methods with  different dynamical methods  and to see how those can help with the  data analysis you suppose you can make a  map  of an entire small brain what branch of  mathematics do you need to simulate it  or to analyze the network  well there's so much mathematics out  there right and this is something that i  hope in the coming years  uh we at mit can make a concrete  contribution to so for example  um last year well before before covet  hit  i met with the director of mit's new  college of computing  um and we started asking hey are there  ways to build more connections between  fundamental computer science and brain  data and these are early stage  discussions  um but it's very clear that  to extract the meaning from biological  data is challenging  but let's look at past successes right  i'd love to look at the history of  biology and medicine  because it can sometimes give you a  little bit of a hint to the future  and one of the areas of great success  has been genomics  so you know first people sequence a  small genome  a virus then there was a big push to  make the human genome but it cost like a  billion us dollars to do that  then next gen sequencing came along and  now sequencing genomes is  much much much much cheaper and then  finally we have bioinformaticists who  are  analyzing lots of genomes and comparing  them and looking at regulatory regions  and mutations and this is  linked to schizophrenia and that's like  to and so on and so forth  um uh and and maybe there's something  similar that might be happening in  neuroscience in the years to come  okay it's hard to analyze the brain but  here's a way to do it  let's do it for a small brain hey let's  scale it up by building  what the next gen equivalent of next-gen  sequencing  and now let's start comparing and mining  and analyzing it  and so my hope is over the next five to  ten years that kind of  innovative culture which is a very  mit-ish thing to do  will emerge and um and that  the kinds of problems in neuroscience  that we currently look at and say wow  that looks pretty impossible  will start to to fall into the realm of  the tractable  the next question i see is about whether  the type of work that you do might make  cognitive neuroscience obsolete and i'm  sure that you  have some interesting insight to that  because you have such close colleagues  in the mcgovern institute who are  cognitive scientists  and how do you see your work interfacing  with theirs or  will you know if you understand every  last bit of  how each molecule works in the brain  would it make cognitive neuroscience  obsolete  i think cognitive neuroscience is very  very important and let me give you an  example of why i think even if you could  map  every molecule and circuit you still  need  a way of thinking about the function  suppose  we see a pattern of activity in the  brain um we see a sequence that  occurs over and over again what does it  do  how do we know whether it's important or  just some random noise  and so i think what we need is better  behavioral neuroscience  and better technologies to um  to go after what you might call meta  behavior these internal states that  contribute to behavior  but the linkage might be remote you  suppose i'm daydreaming right now which  i'm not  but suppose i'm daydreaming i might just  be sitting here and nobody by watching  me will know what's going on in my brain  but three days from now maybe some  imagined idea i had will change my  behavior i'll bet  i was daydreaming about you know some  crazy idea and bam now suddenly i go and  do it right  so it's very clear that behavior is  important and i would say the majority  of neuroscientists right now are very  behavioral  but i think we also have to look inside  and look at internal states  and look at representations that are not  immediately linked to behavior and  that's where the cognitive science is so  important  so to summarize if we care about  function we care about behavior but  behavior is just the tip of the iceberg  right there's that roiling sea of  thought and emotion  that happens all the time only a small  part of which ever rises to the level of  overt behavior we see somebody move or  do something  and i think we need to build much better  internal representations of what's going  on in the brain and so  yeah across mcgovern and and throughout  the brain and cognitive science  department  i think there's a very fruitful  intersection between the behaviorists  the cognitive and internal process  focused people  the circuitry people and hopefully more  and more of the mechanistic molecular  stuff as well  although as noted earlier new tools are  probably needed to help stitch it all  together  and probably our last question because  of time is  someone asks about how you see the  future of treating brain disorders some  combination of dbs  and pharmacology that are already known  to work something totally new  um yeah great question  well there haven't been any cures for  brain diseases  in the sense that you know you can give  somebody penicillin and  uh you know the the infection will go  away  um yeah it's a good question  i think it's a difficult thing to answer  because  very often  the deep complexity of the brain means  we're going to be surprised  i mean if you told me 10 years ago that  i would be helping  a group with trials of effectively  movies for alzheimer's i would say wow  that sounds pretty ludicrous  but you know you know it's like the  the the four-part story i mentioned we  invent new tools people try things out  make discoveries then we help design  practical versions and then they get out  into the world you know that  that that leads to a different way of  thinking  let's take a step back you know i think  a lot of people think like basic science  right  i'm going to discover something or  translational work we're going to cure  people  i would argue there's a third angle  which is innovation  built a tool to accelerate the science  and put the translational work on  firmer ground but it could lead to very  unpredictable outcomes  and i think we're going to find is that  the future of neural therapeutics is  going to be  full of serendipity and full of very  unpredictable outcomes that will help  people  thank you so much i'm going to turn it  back over to wissam to say his uh thank  yous and goodbyes  great thank you jill and uh just leaves  me with  very little to say but just a big big  big thank you to  both jill critterton and professor ed  boyden  for just a fantastic talk i i know i  speak for a number of you or i say  i can't remember the last time i've  learned so much in such a short period  of time personally  and i genuinely mean that so thank you  professor  big thank you to all of you for joining  from the club of great britain and all  around the different clubs in europe and  um and i i know we're going to try  really hard when the world goes back to  normal at some point  to get professor boyden over  to the uk on one of his trips and do a  talk in person uh he's  i know he's in the uk a lot you said  professor for for other  reasons and so we'll try to catch you on  one of those  um again just to close thank you very  much everybody  and if you have any follow-ups or any  feedback or anything please don't  hesitate to email the  mit club of gb and we'll make sure we  get the  questions or thoughts or feedback to the  to the right person  have a very good morning afternoon or  evening take care
 

